#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d2cb7f5f-ad04-5e04-b2da-0f9b555442a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10974" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=5+3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/72/7202d8cbdd95abf0bce85cd66490b29abe5737259def341fcb3202a06c6140a0.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=5+3x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ (0) & =5+3(0) \\\\ 0 & =5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, ![](images/9b/9b7dd489c2bd902529876d678dd3e21d3503f1d42aafba6aa6b3c40dad8f61da.svg) and ![](images/91/911a47012bbfefca8e19f14c023c3924a5e6e02470d6825ad8e4525816bcf2a2.svg) from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+(-1) \\\\ 5+2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, ![](images/db/dbbce97422b8e84b3408bc64d43a250bcba2bd5f2ba5ea5a6aa282e884fa8750.svg) since substituting the coordinates into the equation $y=5+3x$ gives the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ 7 & =5+3(-1) \\\\ 7 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d0d76105-c49e-5cf8-af57-bd74989e895b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10977" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(1,2,2)$ and $(2,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/cd/cd632a2491479baa770168dc5d143944fa72fe7264f27ee51e5518f9e9b9cbe2.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n{x-1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n{(0)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}.  \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n![](images/c5/c58eba4b7130df4c9e588d2792fc1cb1f2b465504ae0859edd213735ca690c97.svg)\nHowever, the point $(3,3,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n{(3)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d2199de1-4bfe-5589-95dd-f9bb7c339437" :question/math-academy-id "e-10980" :question/problem "Let $\\Pi$ be the plane with equation $x+4y-3z=2$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $\\Pi$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $\\Pi$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $\\Pi$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $\\Pi$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/70/70061c1d04780e7a3db2b0c6b41a91c791f029666dff632f35c0ba8d3fd608b0.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (0)+4(0)-3(0) & =2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider ![](images/d0/d0cf195a83c36291bae09c62f542449734d024fadae23b99cecbb4507f03c430.svg) and ![](images/13/13f8e15407cd0dbdfc080f19f906df1ed4bf984635cb2eb30b01f95d50695d53.svg) Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+1 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (3)+4(1)-3(1) & =2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24252954-787b-5287-ada3-c3c084627cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459561} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea7f730-2ff2-5228-b790-8f8914e58843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24252954-787b-5287-ada3-c3c084627cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459561} :answer-field/id #uuid "558fdbad-eec9-50d1-9396-20fe7193cb95" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f04425c-7df1-58ad-b1e6-f2932fcc8dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459563} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc05fc-1ec1-5c2a-8d1d-3483419a8522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f04425c-7df1-58ad-b1e6-f2932fcc8dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459563} :answer-field/id #uuid "8497150c-19f4-5c2a-ae83-13740f506308" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460648} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22251dfe-d083-5dc0-aef6-975964d95330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459565} {:answer/id #uuid "148689b1-11a7-55ba-b407-5c7f94bd335b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22251dfe-d083-5dc0-aef6-975964d95330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459565} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f6846b-3415-5ec5-80cb-0595a3ec6235" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0514bb18-c8aa-5a5a-a02c-b2ade3e4b60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459567} {:answer/id #uuid "079aa3e4-f3eb-5f67-9f8d-9697c3429fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "079aa3e4-f3eb-5f67-9f8d-9697c3429fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f181aeb8-c359-5992-b7c4-2c5057fd3737" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b679935-b7fe-5b87-9b05-95735d1a350e" :question/math-academy-id "q-155957" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2,2,-2)$ and $(3,-4,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{-6}=\\frac{z+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,-2,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(1)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(-2)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "398c8456-89a1-5b2a-b177-7c042901d158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464438} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff8260e-6af0-52e3-bb10-d965f0c5153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff8260e-6af0-52e3-bb10-d965f0c5153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464439} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8f499f9-88c4-51cf-b7cc-fce0e2052fbd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465153} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b69d56-1ac2-5658-9220-6daa7ad09e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464440} {:answer/id #uuid "3902b043-29bf-5320-92e3-81580b55d422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3902b043-29bf-5320-92e3-81580b55d422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464441} :answer-field/id #uuid "3373aec6-7201-50eb-aefe-ad0080fb7754" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f78f89e-a16f-5632-97f4-b5609621130b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464442} {:answer/id #uuid "7947bcec-220d-561f-a8dd-3f1053459776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7947bcec-220d-561f-a8dd-3f1053459776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464443} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2bf7047-04e8-5d42-b4cd-8c904a7e2b18" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84c6930c-489d-5bd2-8a89-92301e886857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464444} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd70689-73bb-5018-9cb5-e10d2dd592c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbd70689-73bb-5018-9cb5-e10d2dd592c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464445} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7476c97-72af-5a74-aead-e576226375b1" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41028c0a-ba2e-5172-bd8b-8e9a6506adda" :question/math-academy-id "q-156596" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=-x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $y=-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\ (0) & =-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a088cc-b42a-52bc-92eb-bd25ac5895d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449708} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae9d0d2-5225-533c-916d-258badd95105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a088cc-b42a-52bc-92eb-bd25ac5895d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449708} :answer-field/id #uuid "937dcbfa-8dc3-50f6-a185-ae4dc328b41b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "326c98a9-ff77-5b6e-aecb-8e7f1dddecc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449710} {:answer/id #uuid "ac781d5c-11d4-539a-a6a0-03e56d803d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "326c98a9-ff77-5b6e-aecb-8e7f1dddecc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449710} :answer-field/id #uuid "c513012e-f0c7-59bd-93e1-bc2abf1598e4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450809} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a8c64f-0c9c-5370-8df5-0f3072768ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449712} {:answer/id #uuid "aced70ec-42bb-5c36-a298-f7b0a764a07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31a8c64f-0c9c-5370-8df5-0f3072768ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449712} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c508d60-bfd9-5766-b225-3dfedd6417f5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450810} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66001218-3dc4-59d1-a926-442ef428f82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449714} {:answer/id #uuid "67502b01-affc-5927-8b15-bd8da2993526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67502b01-affc-5927-8b15-bd8da2993526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449715} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce7a4fe0-4bec-5040-8666-8761d884ec88" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "238a8f80-b240-55d1-a1f3-be7bf8e77e18" :question/math-academy-id "q-156624" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=2y-1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x=2y-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(1)-1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564075} {:answer/id #uuid "66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564131} {:answer/id #uuid "7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564154} {:answer/id #uuid "daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564316} {:answer/id #uuid "f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564316} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-156650" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $3x+y=4$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564029} {:answer/id #uuid "3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564068} {:answer/id #uuid "611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564119} {:answer/id #uuid "7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564158} {:answer/id #uuid "c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564068} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/math-academy-id "q-156682" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x+y=1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaade65c-fd86-5a9b-8729-a4c2c4ccca8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449716} {:answer/id #uuid "bb833c56-c66e-5718-b471-3e56c85a86c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaade65c-fd86-5a9b-8729-a4c2c4ccca8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449716} :answer-field/id #uuid "0011bc07-f81e-5e05-bcdc-0342a9c6c596" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a882b8f-e425-5991-861f-e7c1ca194fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449718} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f9d115-1d82-547b-8423-7b8f79166139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a882b8f-e425-5991-861f-e7c1ca194fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449718} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c51f83-7f86-5f22-a252-185add215172" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450813} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c32c72-3b8d-5eb8-b63b-525c4b6f5be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449720} {:answer/id #uuid "082ec2d9-95d9-56a3-9e45-c48c7dfe1465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31c32c72-3b8d-5eb8-b63b-525c4b6f5be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449720} :answer-field/id #uuid "579d8368-3de0-5e84-9641-f66bc5dcc737" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcc9c302-3722-57e5-8729-a3c5ced64e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449722} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1945d1-4e98-5897-b692-84f26ca03545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d1945d1-4e98-5897-b692-84f26ca03545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449723} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f7c353-1833-5e2f-a1a0-90570b00960c" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "951ef63e-740e-55a7-b946-00aea8b9f2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-156727" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,2,3)$ and $(3,2,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since the second coordinate of any point on this line satisfies\n\n$$\ny=2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564200} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564229} {:answer/id #uuid "c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564263} {:answer/id #uuid "c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564280} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564280} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-157293" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2x-y=3$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "944e44ff-e533-5ed4-96bb-5a535eaddc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464478} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a34893-198a-5797-a5eb-2dac1413854e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a34893-198a-5797-a5eb-2dac1413854e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464479} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b789df4-0f0f-56cb-9ecd-1817894caa49" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e4fe65-09fc-52db-9ad9-c4b810d95943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464480} {:answer/id #uuid "a57fa40c-c577-5110-ad32-33826b9d5ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a57fa40c-c577-5110-ad32-33826b9d5ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464481} :answer-field/id #uuid "e97038ed-fec6-54a7-be13-57daf6ed6250" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9530189a-66d7-5ec1-bcdd-d8a159b2bb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464482} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecdaacb-8609-5a2d-aeb6-96682093c1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ecdaacb-8609-5a2d-aeb6-96682093c1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464483} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d5022e7-1fa9-59b1-bb96-9869737b531f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94ae52d-af32-5aa4-b47f-703bd283b958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464484} {:answer/id #uuid "def3d7d0-1890-579f-af10-3e07494b2e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def3d7d0-1890-579f-af10-3e07494b2e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464485} :answer-field/id #uuid "627aa078-61dc-5bc5-a57c-68e48233cb15" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f036c2d4-285c-5c5e-a300-6732346f17c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157374" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97742b8a-34cf-5189-af20-df43023c61dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464494} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae0d77e-ad6b-5f6a-9374-9d4e224b4416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97742b8a-34cf-5189-af20-df43023c61dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464494} :answer-field/id #uuid "744a436e-d533-5945-91e9-1188f48f53b0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e608cd-a84d-5934-9295-cbb3b7804914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464496} {:answer/id #uuid "5eddee3a-9355-5dc5-9b2b-9982d99d11a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16e608cd-a84d-5934-9295-cbb3b7804914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464496} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6756c7-064e-5db1-88b0-027f7aa281d0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "587ce5fe-38e0-52b8-9ed9-30568644f99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464498} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc16eec-a5c1-509c-b0b2-1e2793716ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "587ce5fe-38e0-52b8-9ed9-30568644f99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464498} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b26f90-c8d9-55db-9b15-dcac52fe238a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465183} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feacad16-16da-5992-b7ff-e20c8092b4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464500} {:answer/id #uuid "dda3dadb-3a83-5c87-9790-b31896571ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feacad16-16da-5992-b7ff-e20c8092b4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464500} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0debc5b-2d21-5008-aa37-bbbd1df242a4" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84592f87-8902-5e5c-972a-3aba1b1c18c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-157377" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,1,1)$ and $(2,-1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=z-1.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n(0)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (0)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(3,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n(3)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (3)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12dfb173-85de-53de-8bbb-769e3ff393b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471059} {:answer/id #uuid "dee9be9f-4d3b-54bb-b16b-61cd95251fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12dfb173-85de-53de-8bbb-769e3ff393b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471059} :answer-field/id #uuid "98f7413f-d2af-5cef-8961-04ae15531969" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472035} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5daac8d8-5f6f-5451-86d9-365c0f6282a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471061} {:answer/id #uuid "c23b81bd-b600-5fba-9034-aa425ea45b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5daac8d8-5f6f-5451-86d9-365c0f6282a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471061} :answer-field/id #uuid "359f2089-4bbe-5fdf-a7c3-8c747afc5255" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b3e9f5-b9e7-5b2c-b9df-8010d612b348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471063} {:answer/id #uuid "68ee5432-10cb-51f0-885c-08f97a2fa22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69b3e9f5-b9e7-5b2c-b9df-8010d612b348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471063} :answer-field/id #uuid "659695e3-c6ca-568b-aa80-eef126aeff96" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71fc54c9-49f4-50e5-81f0-4f589a575427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471065} {:answer/id #uuid "feb87bed-8997-5d53-80a5-8aa6ed79649d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71fc54c9-49f4-50e5-81f0-4f589a575427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471065} :answer-field/id #uuid "c353328a-e322-53ed-a610-69474e8dc78a" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55724e97-d014-5bc4-825e-d54d7bbd24c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157462" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,-1,2)$ and $(1,2,0).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-2}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n(0)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{3}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,1,2)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n(1)\\ne \\frac{(1)+1}{3}\\ne \\frac{(2)-2}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7abbf7e3-3d54-5120-940b-09c5e185eeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464510} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf4fa89-8708-540b-8885-0c47fdd3e072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf4fa89-8708-540b-8885-0c47fdd3e072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464511} :answer-field/id #uuid "557f3350-055d-5356-ae63-38b2200cd37a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c5cfe7-8966-5eae-8257-b7176adfa493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464512} {:answer/id #uuid "9903f099-adbf-5217-b6de-78c54266cd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9903f099-adbf-5217-b6de-78c54266cd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464513} :answer-field/id #uuid "330de474-2c09-5c0b-bf01-776c3b12aa06" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465190} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83483ad5-c53b-598d-8a53-972a1e72c631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464514} {:answer/id #uuid "d31bafaf-13b0-54db-8f23-7a5c4064f4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d31bafaf-13b0-54db-8f23-7a5c4064f4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464515} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7929bb8-48e8-5ad5-a9f9-a247e206819e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a5a7e9-ab0e-54c8-8d41-e44b3cc2bfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464516} {:answer/id #uuid "d18b82a3-c24b-5e6a-aa60-1381535b5797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a5a7e9-ab0e-54c8-8d41-e44b3cc2bfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464516} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bdeead7-751f-5041-8724-a9d669e4d63b" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42d6a94c-6a33-5351-adc5-bba146953de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157463" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2(k{a}_{1})-k{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uu